WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.760
This drop of pond water looks empty.

2
00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:12.260
Is it? And if tiny living things swim in it, why can't your eyes see them?

3
00:00:13.420 --> 00:00:15.600
To answer, we need one test of size.

4
00:00:16.683 --> 00:00:18.283
Start with a metric ruler.

5
00:00:19.643 --> 00:00:22.023
Its smallest marks are one millimetre apart.

6
00:00:23.403 --> 00:00:26.743
Now split one millimetre into a thousand equal parts.

7
00:00:28.003 --> 00:00:29.983
Each tiny part is one micrometre (µm).

8
00:00:31.067 --> 00:00:32.547
Your eyes have a limit.

9
00:00:33.887 --> 00:00:39.447
Most people cannot make out anything smaller than about 100 µm, a tenth of a

10
00:00:39.447 --> 00:00:43.967
millimetre. We will draw that limit as a blue line, the eye line.

11
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:46.560
Now build a size ladder.

12
00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:50.680
Each rung is ten times bigger than the rung below it.

13
00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:54.760
We place real cells on the ladder at their real sizes.

14
00:00:55.883 --> 00:00:59.543
A frog egg is one cell, often about a millimetre across.

15
00:01:00.883 --> 00:01:03.443
It sits above the eye line, so you can see it.

16
00:01:04.523 --> 00:01:08.563
A human egg, about one hundred micrometres, sits right on the line.

17
00:01:09.823 --> 00:01:14.063
A green alga, a red blood cell and a bacterium all sit below it.

18
00:01:14.983 --> 00:01:19.283
Most cells are somewhere between about one and one hundred micrometres.

19
00:01:20.603 --> 00:01:24.663
That puts most of them below the eye line, too small to see without help.

20
00:01:25.683 --> 00:01:29.503
A microscope uses lenses to make small things look larger.

21
00:01:30.983 --> 00:01:35.223
Magnification is how many times larger the picture looks than the real thing.

22
00:01:36.363 --> 00:01:40.143
So the picture looks as big as the real size times the magnification.

23
00:01:41.533 --> 00:01:45.373
Turn the dial up, and every picture slides up the ladder together.

24
00:01:46.653 --> 00:01:47.973
The cells do not change.

25
00:01:49.253 --> 00:01:50.473
Only their pictures grow.

26
00:01:51.873 --> 00:01:52.993
Watch the red blood cell.

27
00:01:54.213 --> 00:01:58.473
At 40x, its picture is already over 300 µm.

28
00:01:59.813 --> 00:02:01.153
It has crossed the eye line.

29
00:02:02.167 --> 00:02:05.407
Now the green alga, about 20 µm wide.

30
00:02:06.687 --> 00:02:08.347
Set the dial to just 10x.

31
00:02:09.547 --> 00:02:11.267
Will its picture cross the eye line?

32
00:02:17.733 --> 00:02:23.353
Yes. Its picture is 200 µm, twice the eye line.

33
00:02:24.493 --> 00:02:28.553
The red blood cell, at the same 10x, still sits below the line.

34
00:02:29.617 --> 00:02:34.417
A school microscope magnifies 40, 100 or 400 times.

35
00:02:35.617 --> 00:02:38.737
At 400x, most cells pass the eye line.

36
00:02:39.977 --> 00:02:44.017
Then you can see their shapes, if the lens shows them clearly and they stand out.

37
00:02:45.017 --> 00:02:47.297
So a clear drop is not always empty.

38
00:02:48.577 --> 00:02:53.077
Most living things in pond water sit below the eye line, where our eyes cannot

39
00:02:53.077 --> 00:02:58.357
reach. That is why most cells stayed hidden until microscopes were made.
